  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 405 AM MST Fri Nov 27 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nDeeper daytime mixing through the afternoon hours on Friday will\nkeep winds brisk across the plains before tapering off around \nsunset. Most plains locations will remain below warning \nthresholds, while Judith Basin County holds onto a more persistent\npressure gradient and mountain wave risk from this afternoon into\nearly Friday afternoon. \n\nAlong the Continental Divide and the mountains of Southwest\nMontana, isolated to scattered snow showers will continue through\nthe morning with up to 2 inches of additional snow possible at\npass level and up to 4 inches possible along the higher peaks.\nSnow showers will briefly let up through the late morning to mid-\nafternoon hours before attention then turns to an upper-level \nshortwave diving southeastward out of British Columbia late Friday\ninto Saturday, inducing a surface low south of the Montana border\nthat tracks due east from Idaho into Wyoming. As zonal flow \ndevelops aloft and a tighter thermal gradient sets up on the \nnorthern side of the low, low- to mid- level shear aligns straight\neast-to-west, creating potential for stationary, training snow \nbands and localized frontogenesis across southwest Montana. Snow \nlevels start around 5,000 to 6,000 feet Friday afternoon before \ndropping to valley floors by Saturday morning, though \nprecipitation tapers off quickly by mid-morning. Where heavier \nbands organize, dynamic cooling will help temperatures fall faster\nand support heavy snowfall rates at times, even at lower \nelevations. Once the system exits and colder air rushes in \nSaturday morning, rapidly dropping temperatures will quickly \nfreeze lingering wet snow and slush on roadways. \n\nRidging builds east across the region on Sunday before weakening as\nzonal flow and Pacific moisture return. Early Sunday stays dry, \nleading into an unsettled start to next week with near- average \ntemperatures and mainly light precipitation favored near the \nContinental Divide.   -RCG/thor\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nHigh Wind Warning through Friday morning:\nNo changes were made to the high wind warnings at this time. There\nis still enough confidence in strong winds to keep them out but\nthere is the potential that the strongest winds may taper off\nsooner than currently expected, especially along the Rocky\nMountain Front. Breezy conditions will still persist along the\nnorthern plains through the day even after the high wind warnings\nexpire, but it is generally expected to remain below high wind \ncriteria.\n\n\nWinter Weather Advisory Friday night into Saturday morning:\nNo changes were made to the current Winter Weather products.\nCompared to the previous forecast, there is still a concern for\nsnowbands to set up but there is still not enough confidence in\nthe intensity or additional snowfall amounts to warrant any\nproduct changes at this time. One wild card to watch will be \nBozeman proper which may benefit from upsloping just enough to \ncause advisory-level impacts and snowfall amounts. At this point \nin time, confidence is too low to expand the current Winter \nWeather Advisory, however, this will be something to watch as the",
